# [K72] `\h`/`\v` (and `\H`/`\V`, and their in-class forms) under `-e utf8` are
# PCRE2_UTF's lists, not the byte sets — the fix for docs/dev/known_issues.md
# K72, found by lane ucpu1 ([UCP] U1, 2026-09-28).
#
# THE LISTS (libpcre2 10.46 under PCRE2_UTF, no UCP; `docs/dev/lanes/
# ucpu1_evidence/probe_hv_10.46.txt` for the U+3000 witness, the rest read
# from the oracle for this file):
#   \h  U+0009 U+0020 U+00A0 U+1680 U+180E U+2000-U+200A U+202F U+205F U+3000
#   \v  U+000A-U+000D U+0085 U+2028 U+2029
# Under `byte` they stay [\t \xa0] and [\n\x0b\f\r\x85] (section 3, the control).
#
# WHAT EACH SECTION COVERS.
#   1. EVERY member of both lists, and 24 non-members chosen at the lists'
#      edges (one below/above each range, the neighbouring Z/C code points,
#      an ASTRAL one), against `^\h$ ^\H$ ^\v$ ^\V$` and the bracketed forms.
#   2. The escapes inside a longer match — a class WITHIN a run, so a
#      multi-byte member is consumed whole and the reported span is in bytes.
#   3. The byte-encoding control: the fix must not widen `\h`/`\v` there.
#
# ORACLE: `# pcre2-only` throughout — python `re` has no `\h`/`\v`, and
# `verify_rxt.py`'s subject decoder is byte-oriented (U14), this directory's
# standing exclusion. The oracle for the utf8 cells is libpcre2 with
# PCRE2_UTF|PCRE2_MATCH_INVALID_UTF over well-formed subjects, so it agrees
# with plain PCRE2_UTF on every cell here.

# pcre2-only
pattern ^\h$
encoding utf8
m "\x09" 0 1
n "\x0a"
n "\x0b"
n "\x0c"
n "\x0d"
m "\x20" 0 1
n "\xc2\x85"
m "\xc2\xa0" 0 2
m "\xe1\x9a\x80" 0 3
m "\xe1\xa0\x8e" 0 3
m "\xe2\x80\x80" 0 3
m "\xe2\x80\x85" 0 3
m "\xe2\x80\x8a" 0 3
n "\xe2\x80\xa8"
n "\xe2\x80\xa9"
m "\xe2\x80\xaf" 0 3
m "\xe2\x81\x9f" 0 3
m "\xe3\x80\x80" 0 3
n "\x08"
n "\x0e"
n "\x1f"
n "\x21"
n "\x41"
n "\xc2\x84"
n "\xc2\x86"
n "\xc2\xa1"
n "\xe1\x99\xbf"
n "\xe1\x9a\x81"
n "\xe1\xa0\x8d"
n "\xe1\xa0\x8f"
n "\xe1\xbf\xbf"
n "\xe2\x80\x8b"
n "\xe2\x80\x8e"
n "\xe2\x80\xa7"
n "\xe2\x80\xaa"
n "\xe2\x80\xb0"
n "\xe2\x81\x9e"
n "\xe2\x81\xa0"
n "\xe2\xbf\xbf"
n "\xe3\x80\x81"
n "\xee\x80\x80"
n "\xf0\x9f\x98\x80"

